<%# locals: (chat:, state:, note: nil) %>
<%# Replaced in place every time the writer produces another version, so this
    root element is what each broadcast aims at. `state` is one of three words
    and nothing else — what happened is a sentence, and a sentence handed to the
    state would name a state nothing recognises.

    The state leads because it is the part that changes; the question was asked
    once and is here so that a card reads as an exchange rather than as a
    listing. It sits inside the replaced block for the same reason the state
    does: the state has to come before it, and only one element is aimed at. %>
<div id="<%= chat.script_id %>">
  <p class="state" data-state="<%= state %>"><%= state %></p>
  <% if note.present? %>
    <p class="note"><%= note %></p>
  <% end %>
  <%# Reproduced, not authored — a heading here would give the page's own voice
      to words the sender typed. %>
  <blockquote><%= chat.question %></blockquote>
  <pre><code><%= highlighted(chat.script) %></code></pre>
</div>
